how to use cloudinary in vs code
Since you're building a MERN project, the best practice is:

Frontend (React) → User selects an image.
Backend (Express) → Receives the image and uploads it to Cloudinary.
MongoDB → Stores only the Cloudinary image URL.

Don't upload directly from React until you're familiar with security. Learning the backend upload flow is more useful.

Step 1: Create a Cloudinary Account
Go to Cloudinary
Sign up.
Open your Dashboard.
Copy:
Cloud Name
API Key
API Secret

Example:
Cloud Name: xxxxxxxx
API Key: 123456789
API Secret: abcdefghijklmnop
Step 2: Install Packages

Inside your backend:
npm install cloudinary multer

If you're using Express:
npm install dotenv
Step 3: Create .env
PORT=5000

CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=your_cloud_name
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=your_api_key
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=your_api_secret

Never commit this file to GitHub.

Step 4: Configure Cloudinary

Create:

config/cloudinary.js
import { v2 as cloudinary } from "cloudinary";

cloudinary.config({
  cloud_name: process.env.CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME,
  api_key: process.env.CLOUDINARY_API_KEY,
  api_secret: process.env.CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET,
});

export default cloudinary;
Step 5: Configure Multer

//Create:
middleware/multer.js
import multer from "multer";
const storage = multer.memoryStorage();
const upload = multer({
  storage,
});
export default upload;

//Step 6: Upload Route
import express from "express";
import upload from "../middleware/multer.js";
import cloudinary from "../config/cloudinary.js";
const router = express.Router();
router.post(
  "/upload",
  upload.single("image"),
  async (req, res) => {
    try {
      const result = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        cloudinary.uploader
          .upload_stream(
            {
              folder: "sports-network",
            },
            (error, result) => {
              if (error) reject(error);
              else resolve(result);
            }
          )
          .end(req.file.buffer);
      });

      res.json(result);
    } catch (err) {
      res.status(500).json({
        message: err.message,
      });
    }
  }
);
export default router;

//Step 7: Use Route
import uploadRoutes from "./routes/upload.js";
app.use("/api", uploadRoutes);
Step 8: React Frontend
<input
  type="file"
  onChange={(e) => setImage(e.target.files[0])}
/>

//Upload:
const formData = new FormData();
formData.append("image", image);
await axios.post(
  "http://localhost:5000/api/upload",
  formData
);
Step 9: Response
Cloudinary returns something like:
{
  "secure_url": "https://res.cloudinary.com/xxxxx/image/upload/v123/profile.jpg"
}

//Save only this URL in MongoDB.
Example:

const user = {
  name: "Sahil",
  profileImage: result.secure_url,
};
