For your Sports & Gaming Network

You'll likely upload:

✅ Player profile pictures
✅ Team logos
✅ Team banners
✅ Ground images
✅ Tournament posters
✅ Achievement photos

Cloudinary is a great fit for all of these.

My recommendation

Since this is your first startup-scale MERN project, build the upload flow yourself with Express + Multer + Cloudinary first. Once you're comfortable with that, you can explore features like image transformations, automatic resizing, and folder organization in Cloudinary. This will give you a solid understanding of how file uploads work end-to-end.

why multer creation is required ? can i use with express then also 

Good question. Yes, Multer is still used with Express. In fact, Multer is middleware for Express.

The important question is why.

Why can't Express receive images directly?

Suppose your React app sends an image.

const formData = new FormData();
formData.append("image", file);

axios.post("/api/upload", formData);

The request is not JSON.

It is sent as:

multipart/form-data

Express knows how to parse:

✅ JSON (application/json)
✅ URL encoded forms (application/x-www-form-urlencoded)

But it does not know how to parse:

multipart/form-data

So if you do:

app.post("/upload", (req, res) => {
    console.log(req.body);
    console.log(req.file);
});

You'll get

req.body = {}
req.file = undefined

because Express cannot understand file uploads by itself.

What Multer does

Multer reads the incoming request and extracts the uploaded file.

Without Multer:

React
    │
multipart/form-data
    │
Express ❌ Can't read file

With Multer:

React
    │
multipart/form-data
    │
Multer
    │
Express
    │
req.file

Now you can do

console.log(req.file);

and get

{
  originalname: "profile.jpg",
  mimetype: "image/jpeg",
  size: 345678,
  buffer: <Buffer ...>
}
Why memoryStorage()?
const storage = multer.memoryStorage();

This tells Multer:

Don't save the file on my computer. Keep it in RAM.

Then you immediately upload it to Cloudinary.

User
   │
Image
   │
Multer (RAM)
   │
Cloudinary
   │
Done

No temporary files are left on your server.

If you don't use memoryStorage

You can also use disk storage.

const storage = multer.diskStorage({
    destination: "./uploads",
    filename: ...
});

Flow:

User
    │
Image
    │
Server uploads/
    │
Cloudinary
    │
Delete local file

This works, but it's slower and leaves temporary files unless you delete them.

Can I upload to Cloudinary without Multer?

Yes, but not in the typical Express backend flow.

Method 1 (Recommended for MERN)
React
     │
Express
     │
Multer
     │
Cloudinary

This is what most MERN apps use.

Method 2

Upload directly from React to Cloudinary using an unsigned upload preset.

React
     │
Cloudinary

No Express.

Pros:

Simpler
Faster

Cons:

Less control
You expose an upload preset
Not ideal for learning backend
For your Sports & Gaming Network

You're building:

Player profiles
Team logos
Ground photos
Tournament banners

A professional flow is:

React
    │
Express
    │
Multer
    │
Cloudinary
    │
MongoDB (save image URL)

This gives you control to:

Validate file types (only images)
Limit file size
Authenticate the user before upload
Organize uploads into folders
Save the Cloudinary URL in your database
My recommendation

Since your goal is to learn backend properly, 
use Express + Multer + Cloudinary. It's the standard pattern you'll encounter in many Node.js/Express projects and will 
make it much easier to understand file uploads in future applications.

