Better Architecture (Recommended)

Instead of calling /upload separately, many production apps combine the upload with the registration endpoint.

PlayerRegistration.jsx
        │
        ▼
POST /api/player/register
        │
        ▼
Multer
        │
        ▼
Cloudinary
        │
        ▼
MongoDB
        │
        ▼
Response

So your route becomes something like:

router.post(
  "/register",
  upload.single("profileImage"),
  registerPlayer
);

Inside registerPlayer, you upload the image to Cloudinary and save the player's data in MongoDB in one request. This is cleaner and reduces unnecessary API calls.

For your Sports & Gaming Network, I recommend this second approach. It's how many real-world MERN applications handle profile image uploads during registration.

const formData = new FormData();

formData.append("image", image);

await axios.post(
  "http://localhost:5000/api/upload", // yo ta local host bha ma ta online ma deploy garna lakoo  
  formData
);

Excellent question. 👍 This is something every MERN developer runs into.

The short answer is: localhost is only for development. When you deploy, you'll use your backend's live URL instead.

During development

Frontend:

http://localhost:5173

Backend:

http://localhost:5000

So you might write:

await axios.post(
  "http://localhost:5000/api/upload",
  formData
);
After deployment

Suppose you deploy:

Frontend (e.g. on Vercel):

https://playlink.vercel.app

Backend (e.g. on Render):

https://playlink-api.onrender.com

Then the request becomes:

await axios.post(
  "https://playlink-api.onrender.com/api/upload",
  formData
);
The professional way: Use environment variables

Don't hardcode URLs in your code.

.env.development
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5000/api
.env.production
VITE_API_URL=https://playlink-api.onrender.com/api

Then in your React code:

const API = import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL;

await axios.post(
  `${API}/upload`,
  formData
);

Now:

During development → http://localhost:5000/api/upload
After deployment → https://playlink-api.onrender.com/api/upload

No code changes are needed—only the environment variable changes.

Even better: Create one Axios instance

Create a file like:

src/api/axios.js
import axios from "axios";

const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL,
});

export default api;

Now everywhere in your app:

import api from "../api/axios";

const formData = new FormData();
formData.append("image", image);

await api.post("/upload", formData);

This is cleaner because if your backend URL changes, you update it in one place.

For your Sports & Gaming Network

Since you're planning a fairly large MERN project, I recommend this structure:

frontend/
│
├── src/
│   ├── api/
│   │   └── axios.js
│   ├── services/
│   │   ├── authService.js
│   │   ├── playerService.js
│   │   ├── teamService.js
│   │   ├── uploadService.js
│   │   └── tournamentService.js
│   └── ...
│
├── .env.development
├── .env.production

This is a scalable pattern used in many production React applications.


Why app.use() instead of app.get()?
app.get()

Used for one specific GET route.

Example:

app.get("/users", (req, res) => {
  res.send("All Users");
});

This only handles:

GET /users
app.use()

Used to mount middleware or a router.

Example:

app.use("/api", uploadRoutes);

If uploadRoutes contains:

router.post("/upload", ...);

Then Express automatically creates:

POST /api/upload









Think of handleSubmit() like this
const handleSubmit = async () => {

    // 1. Validate

    // 2. Create FormData

    // 3. Add all fields

    // 4. Call backend

    // 5. Handle response

}

Notice I didn't mention Cloudinary.